    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d211">

                <pb facs="0418" n="384" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg384"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/7–952</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The United States Deputy Representative at the United
                        Nations (Gross) to the
                        Assistant Secretary of State for United Nations Affairs (Hickerson)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret<lb/>eyes
                            only for mr. hickerson</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New
                                York</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1952-07-09"
                            >July 9, 1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Jack</hi>: You will undoubtedly wish to have this report for your
                    record in response to your letter of July 7<anchor n="1" xml:id="d211-f1"/>
                    regarding the follow-up on the Zinchenko
                    conversation of June 27.</p>
                <p>As I reported to you on the telephone, my efforts to see him were not successful.
                    When I ascertained that he was not planning to attend the Security Council
                    meeting, I asked Jack Ross to phone him to say that I would
                    like to say good-bye to him and have a few words with him. This request, which
                    was an unprecedented one on my part, undoubtedly was sufficient to tip him off
                    that I had something in mind to talk to him about and he should have realized at
                    once what the subject was.</p>
                <p>After telling Jack Ross that he was very busy preparing for
                    his departure from New York, he sent word to me later in the afternoon via Dr.
                        Protitch that he was very sorry that he had been unable
                    to see me, but he had had to rush away.</p>
                <p>I think it is clear that if he had wished to pursue the matter, he could easily
                    have found a few minutes to do so. Why he did not wish to do so is purely a
                    matter of speculation.</p>
                <p>You may want to consider the possible desirability of my following up with
                        Malik. I shall, of course, not do
                    anything in that direction unless and until I hear further from you.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                    >Ernie</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
